# Locale Development — Protective Rule

Purpose: keep token usage low during day-to-day development by treating English as the sole editable locale unless the user explicitly requests other locale work.

## Base locale

- `en` is the **source of truth** for all new user-facing strings.
- Add or change copy in English first; never bulk-sync non-English files proactively.
- All user-facing text must be localized, concise, and use appropriate clinical/operational terminology for its context.

## Frontend (`frontend/lib/l10n/`)

| Allowed during routine work | Requires explicit user instruction |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Edit `app_en.arb` only | Edit `app_fr.arb` or any other `app_<locale>.arb` |
| Add new keys to English | Translate or sync French/other ARB files |
| Run `flutter gen-l10n` after English changes | Full locale parity updates across all ARB files |

## Backend (`backend/src/locales/`)

| Allowed during routine work | Requires explicit user instruction |
| --------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Edit `en.json` only | Edit `fr.json` or any other locale JSON |
| Add new keys to English | Translate or sync French/other locale files |

## Explicit override triggers

Update non-English locale files **only** when the user clearly asks, for example:

- "translate French"
- "sync fr locale"
- "update app_fr.arb"
- "implement French localization"

## Invariants

- Do not hard-code user-facing strings outside locale files.
- Clinical, legal, and user-entered data is never auto-translated.
- English remains the runtime fallback when a non-English key is missing.

